  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "The Young&#39;s modulus of a steel wire of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>&nbsp;and cross-sectional area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>11</mn><mn>11</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> . The wire is suspended from its support on a given planet. A block of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> is attached to the free end of the wire. The acceleration due to gravity on the planet is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> of its value on the earth. The elongation of wire is (Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math> on the earth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> ):",
    "image": null
  },
